{"id":"c58c5c93-a17a-45e7-a1a7-bc9cf81af4d4","object":"chat.completion","created":1785028616,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    nodes_by_id = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n.get('parent')\\n        if p is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(p, []).append(n['id'])\\n    retained = set(nodes_by_id.keys())\\n    removed_order = []\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        # check if any children still retained\\n        has_child_retained = any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, []))\\n        if has_child_retained:\\n            continue\\n        # compute removal set (node + all still-retained descendants)\\n        removal_set = set()\\n        stack = [nid]\\n        while stack:\\n            cur = stack.pop()\\n            if cur not in retained:\\n                continue\\n            removal_set.add(cur)\\n            for ch in children.get(cur, []):\\n                stack.append(ch)\\n        # check required symbols after removal\\n        remaining_symbols = set()\\n        for rid in retained:\\n            if rid not in removal_set:\\n                remaining_symbols.add(nodes_by_id[rid]['symbol'])\\n        if not all(sym in remaining_symbols for sym in required_symbols):\\n            continue\\n        # remove\\n        removal_list = sorted(removal_set, key=lambda rid: (-nodes_by_id[rid]['depth'], rid))\\n        removed_order.extend(removal_list)\\n        retained -= removal_set\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': sorted(retained)\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that follows the task description: grammar-guided node reduction. The payload has nodes (each with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility) and required_symbols. The goal is to reduce the tree by removing nodes that are removable, while preserving that all required symbols are still present in the remaining nodes. Also, the root is never removable (removable=false). Need to consider deeper nodes before shallower nodes, use node ID as tie break at equal depth. Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented. Return removed_node_ids (order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted? The spec says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" The output schema requires arrays of strings, but no explicit sort order mentioned in the output schema; however the description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted (maybe alphabetically or by ID order). The removed_node_ids should be in the order they were removed. The algorithm: we have a tree structure from the nodes list. We need to traverse nodes in order of decreasing depth (deeper first), then by ID if depth equal. For each node that is removable, check if removing it would still keep all required symbols present in the remaining nodes (including its children? Note: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we cannot remove a node if it has children that are retained (i.e., not removed). But we are iterating from leaves upward, so children are considered first. After removing a node, its children are also removed (since they are descendants). However, the check \"node with retained children\" means if any child node is still in the tree (not yet removed), we cannot remove the parent. Since we process deeper first, by the time we consider a parent, its children may have been removed already. So the condition: if the node has any child that is still present in the current set of nodes (i.e., not yet removed), then skip removal. Also, removal is tentative: check that all required symbols are still represented in the remaining nodes after removal. Since we remove the node and its entire subtree? Actually, if we remove a node, we remove that node and all its descendants from the graph. But children are already processed; some may have been removed already. Removal of a node should remove that node and any of its descendants that are still present. But if a child is already removed, that's fine. But we need to consider the effect on required symbols: after removing the node and its subtree, do all required symbols still appear in the remaining nodes? That includes the node itself and its descendants. Also, we must ensure we don't remove a node that is itself a required symbol (but required_symbols list only contains symbols, not IDs; a node with a required symbol cannot be removed because that symbol would be lost). The description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" The \"required symbol\" means a node whose symbol is in required_symbols? Or a node that is necessary for some required symbol? The payload has required_symbols list, so we need to check that after removal, all symbols in required_symbols still appear in the set of retained nodes. That automatically prevents removing a node that is the only one with that symbol. But the rule also says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we must check that condition. Also, we cannot remove a node with removable=false. The root has removable=false, so it's safe. For nodes with symbol in required_symbols, removable might be false? In the fixtures, nodes with symbol \"root\" and \"s1\" have removable=false, but \"s1\" appears in n1 and n4, both have removable=false and true respectively? In first payload, n1 (symbol s1) has removable=false, but n2 is symbol s2 removable=true, n4 is symbol s1 removable=true, etc. So we need to ensure that if a node with removable=true has a symbol that is required, removing it may be fine if other nodes have that symbol. So we must check coverage.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Parse case: nodes list, required_symbols.\n2. Build adjacency: for each node, store its children (by parent id). Also map id->node.\n3. Initialize set of retained node ids (initially all ids) and a list removed order.\n4. Sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending (for tie). Use stable sort.\n5. For each node in that order:\n   - if not removable: continue.\n   - if node's id not in retained: already removed (as child of previous removal) - skip.\n   - Check if any of its children are still in retained. If yes, skip (cannot remove parent while child retained).\n   - Simulate removal: compute the set of nodes that would be removed: this node and all its descendants that are still in retained. To compute descendants, we can do a DFS from this node, but only include nodes currently in retained.\n   - After removal, compute the symbols present in retained minus the removed set. Check if all required_symbols are still present. If yes, then proceed: remove that set from retained, add to removed_order in the order of removal (the node itself first? Possibly the order of removal for the subtree? The description: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" The removal order likely should be the order in which nodes are removed. Since we remove a node and its subtree in one step, we need to decide the order within that subtree. The instruction says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That is the processing order, but removal of a parent causes its subtree to be removed. The removal order should be the order in which the decisions were made, i.e., the node whose removal was decided (the parent) is removed, and its descendants are also removed at that time. But the descendants may have been considered earlier (if they were deeper) and might have been skipped because they had retained children or something. Actually, if a parent is removable, and we remove it, then all its descendants are removed regardless of their own removability? The rule \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" applies to removing the node, which includes its subtree. So if children were individually removable, they might have been removed earlier if they passed conditions. But if they were not removed (maybe because they had children retained), then when we remove the parent, they go as well. So the removal order should reflect the actual sequence of removals. Since we process nodes depth-first from deepest, the deepest nodes are considered first. If a deepest node is removable and passes all checks, it is removed individually, and we record that removal. Then we move up. If a parent later is removed, its children that were not removed earlier (because they were conditional on parent?) Actually if parent is removed, children are removed regardless. But if children were already removed individually, that's fine, they are already in removed_order. So we need to be careful not to double-remove. When we simulate removal of parent, we only remove nodes that are still in retained. So we compute the set of nodes to remove (descendants that are still in retained). We add them to removed_order in some order: maybe add the parent first, then its descendants in some order (e.g., depth order). The description says \"Return ... the actual removal order.\" It doesn't specify ordering within a subtree. We can adopt that we add the nodes in depth order (descending) so that deeper nodes appear earlier in the removal order? But they might have been considered already. Since we are processing from deep to shallow, if a deep node was not removed earlier because it had children, it might later be removed as part of a parent removal. So when we remove the parent, we should add those nodes in an order consistent with the processing order: deeper first. So we can sort the removed set by depth descending, then by id. However, the parent might be shallower, but it is the one triggering removal. The removal order might list the parent then children. The problem statement: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" It doesn't specify the order of removal, just the sequence. I'll assume that when a parent is removed, the removal order includes the parent and then its descendants in some consistent order, maybe depth-first. But to be safe, we can follow the natural order: when we remove a node and its subtree, we can add the node first, then the descendants in the order they were originally processed (deep to shallow). But since we are processing deep to shallow, the descendants would have been processed earlier and possibly skipped. Adding them now in that same order (deep to shallow) would put deeper nodes before parent, which might be inconsistent if they were considered earlier but not removed. However, they are being removed now, so their removal time is now. The actual chronological order is that the parent removal triggers their removal simultaneously. But we can simply append them in depth order (descending) after the parent? That would have the parent before its deeper children. That might be fine. The test cases are not provided, so we need to follow the description closely.\n\nThe description: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That is the order of evaluation. \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" I think retained IDs sorted alphabetically or by something? The word \"sorted\" is ambiguous, but likely they want the retained node IDs sorted in alphabetical order (since IDs are strings like \"n1\", \"root\", etc.) or possibly by insertion order. Safer to sort alphabetically.\n\nAlso, we must not remove root or required symbol. The required_symbols are symbols, so we need to ensure at least one node with that symbol remains.\n\nImplementation steps in solve(case):\n\n- Extract nodes list and required_symbols.\n- Build dict id->node, and dict parent_id->list of children ids.\n- Initialize retained = set of all ids.\n- removed_order = list.\n- Build list of nodes sorted by depth descending, then id ascending.\n- For each node in that order:\n   - if node.id not in retained: continue.\n   - if not node.removable: continue.\n   - # Check if any children of this node are still in retained.\n   - has_retained_child = any(child_id in retained for child_id in children_of(node.id))\n   - if has_retained_child: continue.\n   - # Compute removal set: node and all its descendants that are still in retained.\n   - removal_set = set()\n   - traversal stack: stack=[node.id]\n   - while stack: nid=stack.pop(); if nid not in retained: continue; removal_set.add(nid); for child in children_of(nid): stack.append(child)\n   - # Check required symbols: after removing removal_set, do all required symbols remain?\n   - remaining_symbols = set()\n   - for nid in retained:\n       if nid not in removal_set:\n           remaining_symbols.add(nodes_by_id[nid].symbol)\n   - all_present = all(sym in remaining_symbols for sym in required_symbols)\n   - if not all_present: continue.\n   - # Remove: remove removal_set from retained.\n   - # Add to removed_order: we need to add in some order. We'll add the node itself first, then the rest sorted by depth descending then id.\n   - removal_sorted = sorted(removal_set, key=lambda nid: (-nodes_by_id[nid].depth, nid))\n   - removed_order.extend(removal_sorted)\n   - retained.difference_update(removal_set)\n- After processing all nodes, we have removed_order and retained set.\n- Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted(list(retained))}\n\nNote: The initial retained set includes all nodes. However, root is not removable, so it remains. Required symbols must be represented at the end, which is ensured by the check.\n\nEdge cases: nodes with removable false are skipped. The root is removable false.\n\nAlso, note that children that were already removed (e.g., because they were removable and passed checks) are not in retained, so they don't affect the has_retained_child check.\n\nNow we must output the implementation as a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value the Python source code as a string.\n\nMake sure to escape quotes, newlines, etc.\n\nWrite the solve function using only standard library.\n\nWe'll produce the JSON.\n\nAlso need to handle the fact that the input case might contain \"_case_nonce\" and other fields, but we only use nodes and required_symbols. We should ignore other fields.\n\nLet's write code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2088,"completion_tokens":3163,"total_tokens":5251,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2695},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":40},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}